  "summary": {
    "as": "Reported to House",
    "asOf": "2026-10-29",
    "date": "2026-11-12T21:39:34Z",
    "text": "Responsible Cormorant Management and Control Act of 2026\n\nThis bill requires the U.S. Fish and Wildlife Service (FWS) to develop frameworks to manage regional populations of\u00a0double-crested cormorants (a type of waterbird native to North America).\n\nSpecifically, FWS must coordinate with the four\u00a0Regional Flyway Councils to develop\u00a0regional management frameworks for\u00a0double-crested cormorants. Among other elements, each framework must (1) ensure the breeding population is managed at a sufficient level to ensure conservation, (2) include parameters regarding the taking (e.g., capturing or hunting)\u00a0of double-crested cormorants, and (3) consider any documented effects of double-crested cormorants in the region (e.g., impacts on fisheries).\n\nIn developing each framework, FWS must also work with state and tribal game and fish agencies and the applicable Regional Flyway Council to make recommendations for managing\u00a0double-crested cormorants within the\u00a0National Wildlife Refuge System.\n\nFWS must coordinate with the Regional Flyway Councils every five years to survey the population of double-crested cormorants. Additionally, each framework must be reviewed and updated every five years, incorporating results of the most recent population survey. FWS must also report to Congress on the feasibility of issuing hunting licenses to manage double-crested cormorants in accordance with the frameworks.\n\nThe requirements of this bill terminate on January 1, 2034."
  },
